: [{"turn": 5, "text": "E5 in the centre was the first real slip. Black went from -0.0 to -3.4, a loss of 3.3 points, while the engine preferred F6."}, {"turn": 12, "text": "White's E6 pushed up against Black's D5-E5 stones and lost 8.0 points. The score swung from -4.2 to +3.9, the only time Opus 5.5 was clearly ahead."}, {"turn": 13, "text": "Opus 5.5 played D5 and lost 8.2 points, dropping from +3.9 to -4.3. The engine wanted F6, and White took that point on the next move. A one-move lead was gone."}, {"turn": 23, "text": "With E2 already cut off by F2, D2 lost another 5.9 points and Black fell from -8.1 to -13.9. Black's stones on the bottom edge were being answered move for move, and the deficit was now too big to catch up on."}, {"turn": 33, "text": "From move 27 on, Black's moves each lost 4.6 to 5.9 points, and E8 here lost 5.7, going from -13.9 to -19.6. White's replies recovered about the same amount each time. The engine's choice was B8 for both sides through this stretch, and the score stayed stuck between -14 and -20 until the resignation."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py flood-fills each group on a pasted board and prints its stones and liberties, and count.py flood-fills empty regions to tally area score with named dead stones. Both only analyze the given position, with no candidate-move generation, scoring of moves, or look-ahead.", "stop": false}}